King's Bounty: The Legend is a 2008 'Tactical role-playing' game and the 'spiritual sequel' to the popular 1990 game 'King's Bounty' by the now defunct New World Computing, Inc.
The game features the traditional turn based combat of Heroes of Might and Magic, while at the same time, have the quest, story telling, and progression system of an RPG game.
King's Bounty: The Legend was made by Russian Developer 'Katauri Interactive' and was published by 1C Company.
The game follows the adventures of a newly ordained knight who is appointed as the Kingdom's 'Treasurer Searcher.' During his travels, he is tasked to deliver a strange and supposedly powerful artifact called the Chest of Rage to the King. Before he can finish his task, the chest, along with its powers, binds itself to the knight.
With this artifact, the knight must unlock its power and use it to defeat an ancient evil that has awoken and who is now seeking to destroy the entire world.
+ Very good graphics and special effects for its age (about 8 years old at the time of this review);
+ Excellent use of classical gameplay mechanics;
+ Addition of new game elements that makes the classic style much more fun;
+ Excellent blend of the 'Heroes of Might and Magic' gameplay with traditional RPG elements;
+ Large variety of creatures to recruit;
+ Relatively large and beautifully rendered game world;
+ Tons of side quests that can keep the player busy, however...(See CON #1);
+ Relatively lengthy game time;
+ Challenging but fair difficulty levels; and
+ Good overall story, however...(See CON #2).
- The side quests can either be really interesting or really boring and mundane;
- The story starts slowly and could potentially bore a few players at the start;
- Very limited dialogue options between the player and the NPCs;
- Limited in fast travel options;
- The game is limited to only 3 classes of heroes.
- Chest of Rage cannot be used during boss battles.
+/- No voice acting apart from the ones in the cinematics;
+/- No multiplayer or hotseat game modes;
+/- Due to its age, there is no widescreen support;
+/- Very limited interaction options with the hero's spouse.
that captures the traditional tactical gameplay of the Heroes of Might and Magic series and blending it pleasantly with several RPG elements thus making for a fun and nostalgic gaming experience.
The game cost $6.99 (or your regional equivalent) at its full price. If the game or the Collector's Pack is on a sale, then any turn based strategy fan should consider this in their collection.
